Tool `Paper Search:search_pubmed` with Parameter {'query': 'IMspire150 melanoma atezolizumab vemurafenib cobimetinib overall survival progression-free survival', 'max_results': 5} on Paper Search succeeded. Result (summarized from 1530 tokens): Synthesis across the five sources
- Context: For BRAF V600–mutant unresectable/metastatic melanoma, two effective first-line strategies exist: immune checkpoint inhibitors (ICI) and targeted therapy (TT) with BRAF plus MEK inhibitors. Given complementary mechanisms, several trials tested “triplet” therapy (anti–PD-1/PD-L1 + BRAF inhibitor + MEK inhibitor) vs TT alone.
- Efficacy from triplet trials: Across three triplet studies (including IMspire150 with atezolizumab + vemurafenib + cobimetinib), duration of response improved significantly; progression-free survival (PFS) and overall survival (OS) were at best numerically improved and not consistently statistically superior.
- Regulatory status: One triplet regimen (atezolizumab + vemurafenib + cobimetinib) has been approved for BRAF-mutant metastatic melanoma.
- Sequencing evidence: Two prospective sequencing trials compared first-line ICI followed by TT at progression versus first-line TT followed by ICI; the SECOMBIT study also tested a “sandwich” approach (brief TT lead-in, then ICI until progression, then TT again). Both trials favored first-line ICI; in SECOMBIT, the sandwich strategy showed a similar benefit.
- Current guidance from these data: Prefer immunotherapy-based first-line regimens—or consider the sandwich approach—for BRAF-mutant melanoma. Whether triplet therapy is superior to ICI (mono- or combo-) remains unresolved and must weigh both efficacy and safety.
- Biomarkers: Exploratory analyses from IMspire150 investigated biomarkers associated with triplet benefit.
- Value: Given high drug costs, the cost-effectiveness of triplet therapy versus doublet TT is uncertain despite PFS gains.

Per-article key points

1) Dummer R; Welti M; Ramelyte E (2023). “The role of triple therapy and therapy sequence in treatment of BRAF-mutant metastatic melanoma. Response to overall survival with first-line atezolizumab in combination with vemurafenib and cobimetinib in BRAFV600 mutation-positive advanced melanoma (IMspire150): second interim analysis of a multicentre, randomised, phase 3 study.”
- Type: Commentary/review.
- Key content: Summarizes the triplet-therapy landscape. Across three trials of anti–PD-1/PD-L1 + BRAF/MEK vs TT + placebo, duration of response improved significantly, but PFS/OS were only numerically better, if at all. One triplet was approved. The hypothesized synergy was not uniformly confirmed; advantages may reflect statistical factors more than biology. Sequencing trials (including SECOMBIT) found first-line ICI superior to first-line TT; the SECOMBIT “sandwich” (8 weeks TT → ICI until progression → TT) produced a similar effect. Current data favor immunotherapy-based first-line treatment, with sandwich as a potential alternative. Superiority of triplet over ICI monotherapy remains to be determined with attention to safety.
- DOI: 10.1186/s12967-023-04391-1
- PubMed: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37543586/

2) Ascierto PA et al. (2023). “Overall survival with first-line atezolizumab in combination with vemurafenib and cobimetinib in BRAF…”
- Study: IMspire150 phase 3, second interim OS analysis.
- Population: BRAF V600 mutation–positive advanced melanoma, first line.
- Arms: Atezolizumab + vemurafenib + cobimetinib vs placebo + vemurafenib + cobimetinib.
- Findings referenced: Primary analysis previously showed improved investigator-assessed PFS with the triplet. This report addresses overall survival with first-line triplet; details are in the full text.
- DOI: 10.1016/S1470-2045(22)00687-8
- PubMed: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36460017/

3) Robert C et al. (2022). “Biomarkers of treatment benefit with atezolizumab plus vemurafenib plus cobimetinib in BRAF…”
- Study: IMspire150 (NCT02908672) biomarker analysis.
- Finding: Confirms significantly improved PFS with the triplet vs doublet TT and explores biomarkers associated with treatment benefit to refine patient selection.
- DOI: 10.1016/j.annonc.2022.01.076
- PubMed: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.ni
