Tool `Paper Search:search_pubmed` with Parameter {'query': 'CheckMate 238 adjuvant nivolumab versus ipilimumab melanoma overall survival update', 'max_results': 5} on Paper Search succeeded. Result (summarized from 1058 tokens): SUMMARIZED CONTENT:
- Overall context: For patients with surgically resected, high-risk cutaneous melanoma, adjuvant systemic therapy aims to eradicate micrometastatic disease and reduce relapse. Historically, interferon-α (IFNα) regimens improved relapse-free survival (RFS), with inconsistent overall survival (OS) benefit; high-dose ipilimumab (CTLA-4 inhibitor) improved both RFS and OS but with substantial immune-related toxicity. More recently, anti–PD-1 therapies (nivolumab, pembrolizumab) have been evaluated and are redefining the adjuvant standard.

- CheckMate 238 (adjuvant nivolumab vs ipilimumab in resected stage IIIB–C or stage IV melanoma):
  - Trial design: Multicentre, double-blind, randomised, controlled, phase 3 study in patients with completely resected stage IIIB–C or stage IV melanoma.
  - Prior findings: Nivolumab significantly improved RFS and distant metastasis-free survival (DMFS) versus ipilimumab.
  - 4-year update (Ascierto et al., 2020; Lancet Oncology; doi:10.1016/S1470-2045(20)30494-0): Confirms sustained, significant improvements in RFS and DMFS with nivolumab vs ipilimumab; reports initial OS analyses and late-emergent safety results with longer follow-up.
  - 5-year update and biomarker results (Larkin et al., 2023; Clin Cancer Res; doi:10.1158/1078-0432.CCR-22-3145): Provides updated 5-year efficacy, indicating sustained benefit of adjuvant nivolumab over ipilimumab, and presents biomarker analyses. (Specific biomarker details are not provided in the abstract.)
  - Clinical implication: Durable superiority of adjuvant nivolumab over ipilimumab in preventing recurrence and distant metastases after resection of stage III/IV melanoma, with maturing OS and safety data and emerging biomarker insights.

- Historical and comparative adjuvant therapy evidence (Agha & Tarhini, 2017; Curr Oncol Rep; doi:10.1007/s11912-017-0594-5):
  - IFNα:
    - Across multiple regimens and trials, most demonstrated RFS improvement; OS benefit was inconsistent and regimen-dependent.
    - High-dose interferon (HDI) RCTs:
      - E1684 (vs observation): HR 0.61 for RFS, 0.67 for OS (benefit attenuated on later updates).
      - E1690 (vs observation): HR 0.78 for RFS, 1.0 for OS.
      - E1694 (vs vaccine): HR 0.67 for RFS, 0.72 for OS.
    - Pegylated IFNα (EORTC 18991; stage III): Reduced relapse risk (RFS HR 0.87) without OS improvement.
  - Ipilimumab (CTLA-4):
    - EORTC 18071 (10 mg/kg, stage III): Improved RFS (HR 0.76) and OS (HR 0.72), but with substantial immune-related toxicities.
    - Ongoing/then-planned: E1609 comparing ipilimumab 3 mg/kg or 10 mg/kg vs HDI.
  - Anti–PD-1 agents:
    - Nivolumab: CheckMate 238 (as above).
    - Pembrolizumab: KEYNOTE-054 and S1404 ongoing at the time of the review.

Key takeaways:
- Adjuvant nivolumab provides sustained, superior RFS and DMFS versus ipilimumab in resected stage IIIB–C/IV melanoma, with benefits maintained through 5 years and biomarker analyses emerging.
- Four-year data include initial OS and late safety assessments; five-year results reinforce durability of benefit.
- Earlier standards (HDI IFNα, ipilimumab 10 mg/kg) improved RFS—and for ipilimumab also OS—but with notable toxicity and/or inconsistent OS; anti–PD-1 therapy has become the preferred adjuvant approach based on efficacy and tolerability profiles.
