 FINAL SOLUTION:
2025-12-01 10:10:38,417 - benchmark.runner - INFO - Here’s a concise, evidence-backed status brief you can share with your team. It synthesizes NASA/CCMC DONKI event records, model runs, and related indices covering late Nov through Dec 1, 2025, and clarifies what (if anything) is likely to affect Earth and upcoming sky events.

Executive takeaways
- A series of east-limb M-class flares on Nov 28–29 and an X1.9 flare on Dec 1, 2025, launched multiple fast CMEs (up to 1837 km/s). Sources were near/behind the Sun’s east limb (≈E70–E90), which strongly reduces Earth-impact likelihood.
- DONKI model runs (WSA–Enlil) and notifications indicate impacts for Parker Solar Probe and Mars; no Earth impact was listed for the Dec 1 halo CME as of the latest data.
- For the same late-Nov/early-Dec window, we could not retrieve new DONKI Geomagnetic Storm (GST) entries due to connection errors. The most recent confirmed major storms in November were earlier (Kp up to 8.67 on Nov 12), driven by earlier CMEs (Nov 7–11).
- A coronal hole high-speed stream (HSS) was logged Nov 28, 14:57Z. HSS can modestly elevate geomagnetic activity, but we do not have corresponding Kp confirmations for Nov 28–Dec 2 from DONKI GST due to the retrieval issue.

What happened on the Sun (late Nov → Dec 1, 2025)
- Nov 28 (UTC)
  - Flares:
    - 05:43–06:00, C5.1 at S18E90; related CME at 06:24Z [DONKI FLR list; CME 43069, 833 km/s].
    - 22:11–22:24, M5.9 at S17E89; related CME at 23:45Z [CME 43090, 938 km/s].
    - Additional M1.6 flare at 23:52 (S15E89).
    Sources: DONKI FLR list 2025-11-28 to 12-02; CMEs below.
  - CMEs (selection with speeds and links):
    - 06:24Z, source beyond E limb (C-flare/filament), 833 km/s (Type C) – https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/CME/43069/-1
    - 14:00Z, N30E90, 525 km/s (Type C) – https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/CME/43077/-1
    - 16:36Z, bright E-front, 1049 km/s (Type O) – https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/CME/43081/-1
    - 23:45Z, S17E89, linked to M5.9 flare, 938 km/s (Type C) – https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/CME/43090/-1
    - Additional faint events: 02:12Z (565 km/s) – https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/CME/43065/-1; 03:36Z (353 km/s) – https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/CME/43073/-1
- Nov 29 (UTC)
  - Flares:
    - 04:14–04:22, M1.5 (S16E89); multiple M1–M3 flares through 21:54 (locations N20E89 to S17E70). Source: DONKI FLR list (2025-11-28 to 12-02).
  - CMEs:
    - 04:00Z, S16E89, 854 km/s (Type C), plausibly linked to the occulted M1.5 flare – https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/CME/43100/-1
    - 04:36Z, S55E40, 441 km/s (Type S) – https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/CME/43140/-1
    - Additional overlapping outflows were noted in DONKI on Nov 27–29 to the NE/E with speeds ~267–685 km/s (summary: https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/CME/43044/-1 et seq. in the 2025-11-27 to 11-28 listing).
- Nov 30 (UTC)
  - Flare: 00:16–00:56, C8.0 at N19E89; related CME at 00:36Z – DONKI FLR list; CME in 11/30 notifications.
- Dec 1 (UTC)
  - X1.9 flare: 02:27–03:05, at N23E90 (east limb). DONKI notification IDs 20251201-AL-001 and 20251201-AL-002 (X1.9 flare).
  - Halo CME: start 02:48Z; analyzed speed 1837 km/s (Type O). Source co-located with the flare (N23E90). DONKI event: https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/CME/43155/-1
    - DONKI CME notification (20251201-AL-003) indicates modeled impact for Mars (no Earth impact listed).

Modeled propagation and targets (WSA–Enlil)
- Nov 27–30 model runs show impacts primarily for Parker Solar Probe and later Mars:
  - 2025-11-27 model ID 43056: PSP arrival ~2025-11-29 04:00Z (no Earth listed).
  - 2025-11-28 model ID 43080: PSP ~2025-11-30 01:04Z; Mars ~2025-12-02 08:00Z.
  - Additional late-Nov runs targeting PSP only (e.g., IDs 43067, 43072, 43075, 43083). Source: DONKI WSA–Enlil listings (e.g., https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/WSA-ENLIL/43080/1).
- For Dec 1 X1.9/halo CME: DONKI notification explicitly references Mars; no DONKI Earth impact entry was available in the retrieved set.

Geomagnetic activity at Earth
- Confirmed major November storms (earlier window for context):
  - 2025-11-12 GST: Kp=8.67 at 03:00Z; 8.33 at 06:00Z (G4–G5-level conditions), linked to CMEs on Nov 7, 9, 10; magnetopause crossing logged 00:59Z on Nov 12. Source: https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/GST/42642/-1 and https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/MPC/42637/-1
  - 2025-11-12 18:00Z GST: Kp=6.0 at 21:00Z; 7.33 at 03:00Z (Nov 13) – https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/GST/42662/-1
  - 2025-11-08 GST: Kp=6.33 at 03:00Z – https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/GST/42533/-1
  - 2025-11-05 GST: Kp=6.00 at 00:00Z; 6.67 at 06:00Z – https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/GST/42455/-1
- Late Nov/early Dec (Nov 27–Dec 2): DONKI GST retrievals returned connection errors during our queries, so we cannot cite official Kp from DONKI for this exact window. However:
  - An HSS was logged on 2025-11-28 14:57Z (https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/HSS/43094/-1), which can elevate geomagnetic activity modestly.
  - No DONKI WSA–Enlil runs in this interval listed Earth as an impact target for the late-Nov E-limb CMEs or the Dec 1 halo CME; notifications highlighted PSP and Mars. This, plus the E90 source longitudes, argues against a significant Earth-directed storm from these specific CMEs.

Implications for this week’s “astronomical events”
- Aurora potential: The late-Nov CMEs were launched from near/beyond the east limb. With DONKI models not listing Earth among impact targets, the probability of a strong geomagnetic storm at Earth from these CMEs in the next 1–3 days is low. Modest geomagnetic variability could still arise from coronal-hole HSSs (one HSS observed on Nov 28), but DONKI did not provide Kp for this exact period via GST due to connection issues.
- Daytime radio (HF) impacts: X1.9 flares fall in NOAA’s R3 “Strong” radio blackout category (X1–X9). Expect shortwave HF degradation/blackouts on the sunlit hemisphere during/near the 02:49Z peak on Dec 1. Reference: NOAA R-scale thresholds (X1–X9 = R3). See SWPC R-scale: https://www.swpc.noaa.gov/phenomena/solar-radiation-storms
- Other sky events (conjunctions, meteor showers, eclipses): These are not physically affected by solar flares/CMEs. The main “connection” for observers is potential auroral visibility during geomagnetic disturbances; otherwise, flares/CMEs do not alter visibility of planetary conjunctions, meteors, etc.

What to watch next (with sources)
- DONKI CME page for the Dec 1 halo CME (1837 km/s) and any new modeling that might add Earth: https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/CME/43155/-1
- DONKI notifications feed (FLR/CME/GST/IPS): https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/
- High-speed stream entry (Nov 28): https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/HSS/43094/-1
- Geomagnetic storm pages (for confirmed Kp indices when available): 
  - Nov 12 (Kp 8.67/8.33): https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/GST/42642/-1
  - Nov 12 18:00Z (Kp 6.0/7.33): https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/GST/42662/-1
- Real-time operational checks (for your nowcast/forecast workflow):
  - NOAA SWPC 3‑Day Forecast & Kp: https://www.swpc.noaa.gov/products/3-day-forecast
  - DSCOVR real-time solar wind (for CME/HSS arrival signatures): https://www.swpc.noaa.gov/products/real-time-solar-wind
  - OVATION aurora model: https://www.swpc.noaa.gov/products/aurora-30-minute-forecast

Why these flares likely won’t drive a strong Earth storm
- Source longitudes were near E90 (limb), e.g., M5.9 at S17E89 (Nov 28) and X1.9 at N23E90 (Dec 1). CMEs launched from the east limb normally propagate eastward, away from the Sun–Earth line. Even a “halo” appearance in coronagraphs can be a projection effect for a very wide CME whose central propagation is off the Sun–Earth line.
- DONKI WSA–Enlil runs and notifications for these events list targets like Parker Solar Probe and Mars, not Earth—consistent with an eastward trajectory.

Data caveats
- Multiple DONKI API queries for Nov 27–Dec 2 GST/IPS/MPC/SEP/RBE returned connection errors during this synthesis, so we’ve relied on:
  - Verified flare/CME event logs with times, locations, and speeds (DONKI FLR/CME entries and links above).
  - Available WSA–Enlil model runs and DONKI notifications naming impact targets (PSP, Mars).
  - Earlier-November confirmed GST entries (with Kp) to show the range of recent geomagnetic response and the causal linkage to CMEs.

Appendix: key DONKI event links cited
- Nov 28 CMEs: 06:24Z (43069), 14:00Z (43077), 16:36Z (43081), 23:45Z (43090), 02:12Z (43065), 03:36Z (43073)
- Nov 29 CMEs: 04:00Z (43100), 04:36Z (43140)
- Dec 1 CME (halo, 1837 km/s): 02:48Z (43155)
- HSS: 2025-11-28 14:57Z (43094)
- GST (earlier November): 2025-11-05 (42455), 2025-11-08 (42533), 2025-11-12 (42642; Kp 8.67/8.33), 2025-11-12 18:00Z (42662)

Bottom line for your team
- The late-November/Dec 1 flare/CME activity has been energetic but primarily east-limb. DONKI modeling/notifications point to PSP/Mars impacts, not Earth, and there are no DONKI GST confirmations for this period in the retrieved data. Expect limited geomagnetic impact at Earth from these specific CMEs; any near-term variability would more likely stem from coronal-hole high-speed streams.
- If aurora visibility is a priority for your upcoming events, monitor SWPC’s Kp forecast, real-time solar wind (DSCOVR), and OVATION. For radio-ops, note that the Dec 1 X1.9 implies R3-level HF impacts on the dayside during the flare peak per NOAA R-scale thresholds.
