How to choose your park this winter (Dec–Feb), based on the data
- For consistently pleasant hiking temps with broad campground options: Joshua Tree (fees $30/vehicle; open 24/7) and Pinnacles ($30/vehicle; east side 24/7). Check JTNP’s flood‑related dirt road closures before you go.
- For iconic scenery and organized programs now: Yosemite (events Dec–Jan; $35/vehicle) but expect higher‑elevation road closures and weekday closures on Mist Trail/JMT segments due to blasting.
- For desert warmth and big landscapes: Death Valley (pleasant in winter; $30/vehicle) but verify current road statuses due to November flooding.
- For coastal forests and ranger walks: Redwood (no entrance fee; weekend Forest Walks Dec–May). Bring rain gear; Drury Parkway is closed to vehicles Dec 1–3 for maintenance.
- For sequoia groves with year‑round camping: Sequoia/Kings Canyon—Azalea Campground is open year‑round (110 sites at $32/night), but many park roads are closed in winter; focus on the Grant Grove area.
- For winter recreation and geothermal features: Lassen has significant winter closures on Hwy 89; plan for snow travel and check conditions.

