Tool `National Parks:getParkDetails` with Parameter {'parkCode': 'goga'} on National Parks succeeded. Result (summarized from 3048 tokens): Golden Gate National Recreation Area (code: goga)
- Designation: National Recreation Area
- State: CA
- Website: https://www.nps.gov/goga/index.htm

Overview
- A 60+ mile, mostly coastal urban park around San Francisco, supporting 19 ecosystems and 2,000+ plant and animal species.
- Experiences: hiking, vistas, picnicking, and rich, layered history spanning Indigenous cultures, Spanish colonialism, Mexican Republic, US military, and San Francisco’s growth.

Location
- Coordinates: 37.85982543, -122.6007386
- Address: Golden Gate National Recreation Area, 201 Fort Mason, San Francisco, CA 94123

Contact
- Phone: 415-561-4700
- Email: goga_socialmedia_goga@nps.gov

Weather and seasons
- Winter: sporadic rain, cool; green hills.
- Blooms: late Feb–May/June.
- Summer (by July): coastal fog common; grasses turn golden/brown.
- Sept–Oct: clear skies and abundant sun.

Directions
- Park sites span north and south of San Francisco; access via Highways 1, 101, 280 (from North/South Bay) and I-80 (from East Bay).
- Park HQ (Fort Mason): enter at Franklin St & Bay St, San Francisco.
- Directions page: http://www.nps.gov/goga/planyourvisit/directions.htm

Fees and passes
- Entrance fees: none listed
- Entrance passes: none listed

Operating hours and site specifics
- General access: Most areas accessible 24/7.
- Parking lots: Generally open sunrise to sunset.
- Day Use Areas (6:00 a.m. to 1 hour after sunset):
  - Marin County: Conzelman Rd (west, between traffic circle and Field Rd), Kirby Cove (except permitted campers), Muir Beach, Muir Beach Overlook, Stinson Beach.
  - San Francisco County: Fort Point (north of the gate and adjacent areas), China Beach, Fort Mason – Black Point Stairway to Van Ness Ave., Lands End, Sutro Heights Park.
- Muir Woods National Monument: Open daily; hours vary seasonally (check Muir Woods website). Example listed hours: 8:00 a.m.–6:00 p.m.
- Point Bonita Lighthouse (beyond the tunnel): Open Mon, Fri–Sun 12:30–3:30 p.m.; closed Tue–Thu.
- Nike Missile Site (Marin Headlands): Open Thu–Sat 12:00–3:00 p.m. for demos/programs.
- Battery Townsley: Open first Sunday monthly, 12:00–4:00 p.m. (guided tours).
- Fort Point National Historic Site: Generally open Fri–Sun 10:00 a.m.–5:00 p.m. (confirm on Fort Point’s website).

Topics and themes
- Built environment: Architecture/building, forts, bridges, lighthouses, ruins, monuments/memorials, national cemetery.
- Heritage and history: African American, Asian American, Hispanic/Latino, LGB, Native American, Women’s history.
- Archeology and arts: Archeology, arts, photography, living history/re-enactments.
- Colonization and society: Colonization/settlement, missions, religion/spirituality, immigration, incarceration (jails/prisons, Japanese-American internment), enslavement, social movements (abolition, civil rights, conservation).
- Exploration and economy: Explorers/expeditions, farming/agriculture, industry (logging/timber), transportation (bridges), urban America, Westward expansion, Gold Rush.
- Maritime: Coastal defenses, maritime military, ships/shipwrecks.
- Military: Infantry/militia, artillery, cavalry, US Army, US Coast Guard.
- Health and events: Medicine/hospitals; tragic events (catastrophic fires, forced marches).
- Conflicts: Tribal conflicts, colonial/European contact conflicts, Mexican War, Indian/Frontier Wars, Civil War, Spanish-American War, World War I, World War II, Vietnam War, Cold War.
- Natural environment: Climate change; coasts/islands/atolls; dunes; estuaries/mangroves; fire; foothills/plains/valleys; forests/woodlands (coniferous); geology; grasslands/prairies; natural sounds; oceans (whales); scenic views; trails; unique/rare/endangered species; watersheds; wetlands/marshes.
- Wildlife: Animals, birds, fish, tortoises/turtles.

Activities
- Arts and culture
- Biking (including mountain biking)
- Camping (car/front-country, group)
- Fishing (saltwater)
- Food/dining, picnicking
