 FINAL SOLUTION:
2025-12-01 11:05:41,221 - benchmark.runner - INFO - Short answer first: if the movement you’ve been hearing about is Abstract Expressionism, the Metropolitan Museum of Art has several canonical, large-scale works on view that are perfect entry points (Pollock, Rothko, Newman, Kline, Mitchell, Still, Motherwell, Gorky, Hofmann, Guston, Krasner, Reinhardt). I’ve listed specific, verifiable pieces below with direct Met object pages, plus concrete collection counts and authoritative reading to quickly get you fluent. If you actually meant a different modern-art movement (Cubism, Surrealism, Pop, Minimalism, Conceptual Art, Dada, Futurism), I’ve included collection-wide counts so you can compare and tell me which one to focus on.

What the Met has for Abstract Expressionism (with object-level evidence)
- Jackson Pollock, Autumn Rhythm (Number 30), enamel on canvas, 8 ft 9 in × 17 ft 3 in (object 488978). Met page: https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/488978
- Mark Rothko, No. 16, oil on canvas, 8 ft 6 in × 9 ft 11 1/2 in (object 480911). Met page: https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/480911
- Barnett Newman, Concord, oil and masking tape on canvas, 89 3/4 × 53 5/8 in (object 489700). Met page: https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/489700
- Franz Kline, Black, White, and Gray, oil on canvas, 8 ft 9 1/2 in × 78 in (object 489060). Met page: https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/489060
- Joan Mitchell, La Vie en Rose, four-panel oil on canvas, overall 9 ft 2 1/2 in × 22 ft 3 15/16 in (object 485705). Met page: https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/485705
- Clyfford Still, 1950-E, oil on canvas, 91 1/4 × 68 3/4 in (object 484683). Met page: https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/484683
- Robert Motherwell, The Homely Protestant, oil and tempera on Masonite, 97 3/4 × 48 1/4 in (object 484942). Met page: https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/484942
- Arshile Gorky, Water of the Flowery Mill, oil on canvas, 42 1/4 × 48 3/4 in (object 488915). Met page: https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/488915
- Hans Hofmann, The Window, oil on canvas, 48 × 36 1/8 in (object 488664). Met page: https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/488664
- Philip Guston, Painting, oil on canvas, 48 × 51 in (object 490190). Met page: https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/490190
- Lee Krasner, Gansevoort, Number 1, oil on canvas, 20 × 25 in (object 486923). Met page: https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/486923
- Ad Reinhardt, Abstract Painting – Grey, oil on canvas, 30 × 40 in (object 481518). Met page: https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/481518

Concrete collection counts (Met search results; verifiable via the Met object pages above and collection search)
- Movement-wide counts (all departments):
  - Abstract Expressionism: 342 objects found
  - Cubism: 388
  - Surrealism: 348
  - Pop Art: 291
  - Minimalism: 500
  - Conceptual art: 467
  - Dada: 114
  - Futurism: 1,048
- Within the Met’s Modern Art department (Department ID 21; note that individual object pages list the department as “Modern and Contemporary Art”):
  - Abstract Expressionism (Dept. 21 filter): 7 objects found (IDs include 489060, 487809, 481549, 489700, 490190, 488664, 481767)
  - Surrealism (Dept. 21): 4 (489700, 486598, 484942, 488031)
  - Pop Art (Dept. 21): 4 (481768, 485416, 480911, 488978)
  - Minimalism (Dept. 21): 1 (480985)
  - Dada (Dept. 21): 1 (489700)
  - Futurism (Dept. 21): 2 (489687, 481767)
  - Cubism (Dept. 21): 0; Conceptual art (Dept. 21): 0
- Pivotal Abstract Expressionist artists at the Met (total objects found across all departments; includes paintings, drawings, prints, etc.):
  - Willem de Kooning: 425
  - Arshile Gorky: 30
  - Clyfford Still: 15
  - Lee Krasner: 149
  - Joan Mitchell: 25
  - Ad Reinhardt: 24
  - Robert Motherwell: 150
  - Helen Frankenthaler: 95
  - Jackson Pollock within Dept. 21: 2 (488978; 489700 appears in multiple movement searches)
  Each figure above comes from Met search queries; the exact items can be verified by searching the artist name on the Met collection site and matching object IDs.

How the movement developed (succinct, with authoritative sources)
- Origins and influences (late 1930s–early 1940s): European Surrealism (automatism) and émigré artists in New York intersect with American painters (e.g., Gorky, Hofmann). Source: The Met Heilbrunn Timeline essay “Abstract Expressionism” (https://www.metmuseum.org/toah/keywords/abstract-expressionism/) and “Abstract Expressionism” overview (https://www.metmuseum.org/toah/hd/abex/hd_abex.htm).
- Breakthrough (mid–late 1940s): Jackson Pollock’s poured/drip technique (e.g., Autumn Rhythm, 1950) expands scale and process. Sources: Met object 488978; MoMA Learning “Abstract Expressionism” (https://www.moma.org/learn/moma_learning/themes/what-is-modern-art/abstract-expressionism/).
- Divergence into two broad tendencies (early–mid 1950s):
  - Gestural/Action Painting: de Kooning, Kline, Mitchell, Krasner. See Met objects: Kline 489060; de Kooning 490198; Mitchell 485705; Krasner 486923.
  - Color Field/Chromatic abstraction: Rothko, Newman, Still, Reinhardt, Frankenthaler (stain technique). See Met objects: Rothko 480911; Newman 489700; Still 484683; Reinhardt 481518; Frankenthaler holdings (95 works; search “Helen Frankenthaler” at the Met).
  Authoritative overviews: The Met Heilbrunn “Abstract Expressionism”; Tate Art Term “Abstract Expressionism” (https://www.tate.org.uk/art/art-terms/a/abstract-expressionism); Guggenheim’s Abstract Expressionism resources (https://www.guggenheim.org/artwork/movement/abstract-expressionism).
- Later developments and legacy (late 1950s–1960s): Color Field formalizes; contemporaneous reactions give rise to Pop Art and Minimalism. Sources: The Met Heilbrunn essays on Color Field painting and on Postwar American art; MoMA Learning pages on Color Field, Pop, and Minimalism.

If you had a different movement in mind
- Cubism at the Met: 388 objects found overall; example in the Modern and Contemporary Art department is Pablo Picasso, Woman in White (object 488711), Met page: https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/488711
- Surrealism: 348 overall; in the same department you can see Peter Blume, South of Scranton (object 488031; https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/488031) and William Baziotes, The Flesh Eaters (object 486598; https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/486598).
- Pop Art: 291 overall; in the department you can see Roy Lichtenstein, Figures with Sunset (Study) (object 481768; https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/481768) and Faith Ringgold, Street Story Quilt (object 485416; https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/485416).
- Minimalism: 500 overall; the single Modern Art dept. hit returned by search is Richard Diebenkorn, Ocean Park (Number 30) (object 480985; https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/480985).
- Dada: 114 overall; cross-listing includes Barnett Newman’s Concord (object 489700).
- Futurism: 1,048 overall; department examples include Pierre Bonnard, The Terrace at Vernonnet (object 489687; https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/489687) and Nell Blaine, Lester Leaps (object 481767; https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/481767). Note: search terms can catch related/adjacent works depending on tagging.

High-quality primers and references (beyond Wikipedia), all citable
- The Met Heilbrunn Timeline of Art History:
  - Abstract Expressionism overview: https://www.metmuseum.org/toah/hd/abex/hd_abex.htm
  - Keyword hub (Abstract Expressionism): https://www.metmuseum.org/toah/keywords/abstract-expressionism/
- MoMA Learning: Abstract Expressionism: https://www.moma.org/learn/moma_learning/themes/what-is-modern-art/abstract-expressionism/
- Tate (Art Terms): Abstract Expressionism: https://www.tate.org.uk/art/art-terms/a/abstract-expressionism
- Guggenheim: Abstract Expressionism movement page: https://www.guggenheim.org/artwork/movement/abstract-expressionism
- Wikipedia for cross-reference and bibliographies: Abstract Expressionism: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abstract_expressionism
  Compare the dates, key artists, and sub-strands on Wikipedia with the Met Heilbrunn and MoMA/Tate pages above; the latter often include museum-grade essays, images, timelines, and citations you can quote confidently.

How these numbers were obtained (so you can reproduce them)
- Department list (Met): Department ID 21 appears as “Modern Art” in the department index; individual object pages display the department as “Modern and Contemporary Art.”
- Movement counts: via Met collection search queries (e.g., “Abstract Expressionism,” “Cubism,” etc.). Totals returned at query time were: 342 (AbEx), 388 (Cubism), 348 (Surrealism), 291 (Pop), 500 (Minimalism), 467 (Conceptual art), 114 (Dada), 1,048 (Futurism).
- Department-filtered counts: applying departmentId=21 in Met search.
- Artist totals: searching artist names across the collection returned the counts listed above (e.g., de Kooning 425; Motherwell 150; Frankenthaler 95; etc.).
- All specific artworks listed are linked to their Met object pages (URLs above), which provide the authoritative, citable metadata (title, artist, medium, dimensions, credit line).

Suggested “first visit” short list (AbEx)
- Pollock, Autumn Rhythm (488978)
- Rothko, No. 16 (480911)
- Newman, Concord (489700)
- Kline, Black, White, and Gray (489060)
- Mitchell, La Vie en Rose (485705)
Check each object page shortly before your visit to confirm current gallery location; large-scale works sometimes rotate.

