- Video-R2: Reinforcing Consistent and Grounded Reasoning in Multimodal Language Models (Maaz et al.)
  - Problem: Video MLLMs produce convincing but logically inconsistent and weakly visual-grounded reasoning.
  - Diagnostics: Introduces two metrics: Think Answer Consistency (TAC) for reasoning–answer alignment; Video Attention Score (VAS) for reliance on visual vs. textual cues. Analysis over 11 video reasoning benchmarks shows heavy reliance on linguistic priors.
  - Method: Dual-stage post-training—timestamp-aware supervised fine-tuning + GRPO (Group Relative Policy Optimization) with a new Temporal Alignment Reward (TAR) to enforce temporal alignment and causal coherence.
  - Results: Consistent gains in TAC, VAS, and accuracy across multiple benchmarks; code/dataset/model to be open-sourced.

- Video-CoM: Interactive Video Reasoning via Chain of Manipulations (Rasheed et al.)
  - Problem: Current MLLMs “think about” videos passively in text, cannot rewatch/refocus/verify, limiting spatio-temporal reasoning.
  - Paradigm: Interactive Video Reasoning—“think with videos.”
  - Model: Video-CoM executes a Chain of Manipulations (CoM) via iterative visual actions to gather/refine evidence.
  - Data/Training: Video-CoM Instruct (18K instruction-tuning samples) for multi-step manipulation reasoning; RL via reasoning-aware GRPO with step-level reasoning rewards (beyond sparse answer rewards).
  - Results: Strong performance across 9 benchmarks; +3.6% average over recent SOTA using only 25K SFT + 3K GRPO videos; ablations show reasoning-aware rewards improve accuracy and interpretability.
  - Code: https://github.com/mbzuai-oryx/Video-CoM

- Thinking by Doing: Building Efficient World Model Reasoning in LLMs via Multi-turn Interaction (Shu et al.)
  - Problem: Rigid multi-turn reasoning limits active learning in world-model building.
  - Method (WMAct): 
    - Reward rescaling based on action efficacy to reduce redundancy and encourage purposeful interaction.
    - Interaction frequency annealing to progressively lower max interaction turns, forcing internalization of dynamics.
  - Results: On Sokoban, Maze, Taxi—solves tasks in a single turn that previously needed multiple; strong transfer and improved performance on reasoning benchmarks.

- AnyTalker: Scaling Multi-Person Talking Video Generation with Interactivity Refinement (Zhong et al.)
  - Challenge: Multi-person talking video generation suffers from limited diverse data and difficulty coordinating multiple identities.
  - Architecture: Extensible multi-stream design; identity-aware attention inside a Diffusion Transformer iteratively processes identity–audio pairs, scaling to arbitrary identities.
  - Training: Primarily from single-person videos to learn multi-person speaking patterns; interactivity refined with a small set of real multi-person clips.
  - Contribution: New metric and dataset to evaluate naturalness and interactivity.
