Tool `Hugging Face:get-paper-info` with Parameter {'arxiv_id': '2101.00027'} on Hugging Face succeeded. Result (summarized from 274579 tokens): Title: The Pile: An 800GB Dataset of Diverse Text for Language Modeling
Authors: Leo Gao, Stella Biderman, Sid Black, Laurence Golding, Travis Hoppe, Charles Foster, Jason Phang, Horace He, Anish Thite, Noa Nabeshima, Shawn Presser, Connor Leahy
Link: https://huggingface.co/papers/2101.00027

What it is
- A large-scale, English-language text corpus (≈825 GiB) designed to train and evaluate large language models (LLMs).
- Built from 22 diverse, high-quality subsets (some existing, some newly constructed), many from academic and professional sources.

Key findings
- Untuned GPT-2 and GPT-3 struggle on several Pile components (e.g., academic writing), indicating these domains are challenging for general web-trained models.
- Models trained on The Pile significantly outperform those trained on Raw Common Crawl and CC-100 across all Pile components, and also improve on downstream evaluations.
- The authors conduct an in-depth exploratory analysis of the data and document potentially concerning aspects for users to consider.
- Code used for construction is publicly available.

Why it matters
- Demonstrates that increased dataset diversity measurably improves cross-domain knowledge and generalization in LLMs.
- Establishes a high-quality, publicly accessible benchmark corpus that has become a foundation for many open LLMs and research efforts.

Notable implementations and adoptions (examples)
- EleutherAI:
  - GPT-J-6B (trained on The Pile)
  - GPT-NeoX-20B (trained on The Pile)
  - GPT-Neo family (125M, 1.3B, 2.7B; trained on The Pile)
  - Pythia family (e.g., 70M–12B, including deduplicated variants; trained on The Pile)
- Cerebras-GPT series (111M to 13B; trained on The Pile)
- Falcon series (7B, 40B, 180B; trained on RefinedWeb but cite and align with Pile-related literature)
- Stability AI StableLM models (various sizes, often trained on mixtures that include Pile-related or successor datasets such as Pile v2 dev)
- Additional references in multilingual/legal/research-oriented models (e.g., BSC-LT families) that include The Pile or its deduplicated form among training sources or cite the paper.

Core takeaways
- The Pile’s diversity enables better performance in specialized domains than web-only corpora.
- It has become a widely used, community-standard dataset for training and benchmarking open LLMs.
- Users should review the authors’ documented caveats and concerns when employing The Pile in training or evaluation.
