- allenai/DataDecide-dclm-baseline-qc-7p-fw2-750M
- allenai/DataDecide-dolma1_7-no-math-code-750M
- allenai/DataDecide-c4-750M
- allenai/DataDecide-fineweb-edu-10M
- allenai/DataDecide-fineweb-pro-8M
- allenai/DataDecide-fineweb-edu-14M
- allenai/DataDecide-c4-16M
- allenai/DataDecide-dclm-baseline-qc-7p-fw2-16M
- allenai/DataDecide-dclm-baseline-75p-dolma1.7-25p-6M (truncated in source list but indicates a 75/25 DCLM/Dolma mix)

Notes on implementations
- Common tags: hf_olmo, en, dataset:allenai/datadecide, license: apache-2.0, region: us, and references to related arXiv works (e.g., 2402.00159 and others).
- Variants probe data curation choices and mixtures: C4, FineWeb (edu/pro), DCLM baselines, Falcon & CommonCrawl with quality control (qc), and Dolma1.7 subsets with ablations (e.g., no-reddit, no-math-code, no-flan).
- Training sizes span from a few million tokens to ~1B tokens to study scaling and data composition effects.

Core takeaway
Dolma provides an unprecedented, openly available 3T-token English corpus plus tooling and documentation to rigorously study how pretraining data curation—filters, deduplication, and source mixing—affects language model performance. It underpins the training of OLMo and is accompanied by a suite of small, public checkpoints exploring diverse data mixtures and ablations.
Tool `Hugging Face:get-paper-info` with Parameter {'arxiv_id': '2009.03300'} on Hugging Face succeeded. Result (summarized from 227977 tokens): Title: Measuring Massive Multitask Language Understanding (MMLU) [arXiv:2009.03300]
Authors: Dan Hendrycks, Collin Burns, Steven Basart, Andy Zou, Mantas Mazeika, Dawn Song, Jacob Steinhardt
Link: https://huggingface.co/papers/2009.03300

Core idea:
- Introduces MMLU, a comprehensive benchmark to measure text models’ multitask accuracy across 57 tasks spanning elementary mathematics, US history, computer science, law, and more.
- High performance requires broad world knowledge and problem-solving ability, testing both breadth and depth of academic/professional understanding.

Key findings:
- Most recent models perform near random chance on average.
- The largest GPT-3 variant outperforms random by ~20 percentage points, but still falls short of expert-level accuracy across all 57 tasks.
- Performance is uneven across subjects; models often lack calibrated confidence and “don’t know when they’re wrong.”
- Accuracy remains near random on socially important topics like morality and law.
- The benchmark helps analyze cross-domain competence and identify critical shortcomings.

Adoption/implementations (examples):
- Widely adopted as a standard evaluation benchmark; numerous models reference/evaluate on MMLU.
- Notable families/models include:
  - Google Gemma series: Gemma (2B, 7B), Gemma-IT variants; Gemma 2 (2B, 9B, 27B, with IT variants); Gemma 3 (270M, 1B, 4B, 12B, 27B, with IT/any-to-any/multimodal variants); Gemma 3n (E2B/E4B preview/IT multimodal).
  - Baichuan models: Baichuan-7B, Baichuan-13B-Chat.
  - Qwen: Qwen-7B-Chat.
- These entries often tag arxiv:2009.03300, indicating evaluation/association with MMLU.

Significance:
- MMLU provides a broad, rigorous metric for general knowledge and reasoning ability, revealing persistent gaps in current LLMs, especially in sensitive domains, and guiding future model improvement.
Tool `Hugging Face:get-paper-info` with Parameter {'arxiv_id': '2110.14168'} on Hugging Face succeeded. Result (summarized from 263315 tokens): Title: Training Verifiers to Solve Math Word Problems
Authors: Karl Cobbe, Vineet Kosaraju, Mohammad Bavarian, Mark Chen, Heewoo Jun, Lukasz Kaiser, Matthias Plappert, Jerry Tworek, Jacob Hilton, Reiichiro Nakano, Christopher Hesse, John Schulman
Link: https://huggingface.co/papers/2110.14168

Core idea and contributions:
- Challenge: While state-of-the-art language models match human performance on many tasks, they struggle with robust, multi-step mathematical reasoning.
- Dataset: Introduces GSM8K, a high-quality, linguistically diverse dataset of 8.5K grade-school math word problems designed to diagnose such failures.
- Baseline finding: Even the largest transformer models fail to achieve high test performance on GSM8K, despite the conceptual simplicity of the problems.
- Method: Train a verifier model to judge the correctness of candidate solutions. At test time, generate many candidate solutions and select the one ranked highest by the verifier.
- Results: Verification significantly improves performance on GSM8K and scales more effectively with increased data than standard finetuning baselines.

