1) Video-R2: Reinforcing Consistent and Grounded Reasoning in Multimodal Language Models (cs.CV)
- Authors: Muhammad Maaz; Hanoona Rasheed; Fahad Shahbaz Khan; Salman Khan
- Problem: Video reasoning in MLLMs often produces convincing but logically inconsistent and weakly visual-grounded traces.
- Diagnostics introduced:
  - Think Answer Consistency (TAC): alignment between reasoning and answers.
  - Video Attention Score (VAS): reliance on visual vs. textual cues.
- Findings: Across 11 video reasoning benchmarks, current models lean heavily on linguistic priors over visual evidence.
- Method: Two-stage post-training—timestamp-aware SFT + GRPO with a new Temporal Alignment Reward (TAR)—to improve temporal precision and reasoning consistency.
- Results: Consistent gains in TAC, VAS, and accuracy across multiple benchmarks; improved trustworthy video understanding.
- Availability: Code, dataset, and model to be open-sourced.
- Links: arXiv: 2511.23478v1; PDF: https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.23478v1

2) Video-CoM: Interactive Video Reasoning via Chain of Manipulations (cs.CV)
- Authors: Hanoona Rasheed; Mohammed Zumri; Muhammad Maaz; Ming-Hsuan Yang; Fahad Shahbaz Khan; Salman Khan
- Problem: Most MLLMs “think about” videos after a single encoding, limiting active visual reasoning and evidence verification.
- Paradigm: Interactive Video Reasoning—models “think with videos,” treating video as an active workspace.
- Model: Video-CoM uses a Chain of Manipulations (CoM) to iteratively gather/refine visual evidence.
- Data: Video-CoM Instruct—18K instruction-tuning examples for multi-step manipulation reasoning.
- Learning: Supervised training + RL via reasoning-aware GRPO with step-level reasoning rewards (not just sparse answer rewards).
- Results: Strong performance on 9 video reasoning benchmarks; +3.6% average over recent SOTA with only 25K SFT and 3K GRPO video samples. Ablations show better accuracy and interpretability from reasoning-aware rewards.
- Code: https://github.com/mbzuai-oryx/Video-CoM
- Links: arXiv: 2511.23477v1; PDF: https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.23477v1

3) Hunyuan-GameCraft-2: Instruction-following Interactive Game World Model (cs.CV)
- Authors: Junshu Tang; Jiacheng Liu; Jiaqi Li; Longhuang Wu; Haoyu Yang; Penghao Zhao; Siruis Gong; Xiang Yuan; Shuai Shao; Qinglin Lu
- Problem: Prior generative world models rely on rigid action schemas and costly annotations, limiting diverse interactions and player-driven dynamics.
- Contribution:
  - Instruction-driven interaction enabling control via natural language, keyboard, or mouse.
  - Formalization of “interactive video data” and an automated pipeline to convert large-scale text-video pairs into causally aligned interactive datasets.
  - Built on a 14B image-to-video MoE foundation with a text-driven interaction injection mechanism for fine-grained control (camera, character, environment).
- Benchmark: InterBench for comprehensive interaction evaluation.
- Results: Generates temporally coherent, causally grounded interactive game videos that faithfully follow free-form instructions (e.g., “open the door,” “draw a torch,” “trigger an explosion”).
- Links: arXiv: 2511.23429v1; PDF: https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.23429v1

4) Variation of Microphysical Parameters in Reverse-shock Scenario (astro-ph.HE)
- Authors: Nissim Fraija; Boris Betancourt-Kamenetskaia; Antonio Galván; Maria Dainotti
- Focus: Time-varying microphysical parameters in GRB reverse shocks and their impact on energy distribution between particles and magnetic fields.
- Theory: Derives SSC light curves and closure relations from external reverse shocks in homogeneous and stellar-wind media; considers thick- and thin-shell regimes.
- Insights: Variation can mimic plateau phases and produce temporal decay steeper than high-latitude emission predictions.
- Application: Uses MCMC to analyze spectral and temporal evolution for GRBs from the Second Fer
