Tool `Hugging Face:get-paper-info` with Parameter {'arxiv_id': '1905.07830'} on Hugging Face succeeded. Result (summarized from 217899 tokens): Title: HellaSwag: Can a Machine Really Finish Your Sentence?
Authors: Rowan Zellers, Ari Holtzman, Yonatan Bisk, Ali Farhadi, Yejin Choi
Link: https://huggingface.co/papers/1905.07830

What the paper studies
- Task: Commonsense natural language inference via sentence completion. Given an event description (e.g., “A woman sits at a piano”), the model must choose the most plausible follow-up.
- Context: Earlier work (Zellers et al., 2018) and models like BERT achieved near-human performance on related benchmarks, raising the question of whether machines truly match human commonsense inference.

Key contributions
- HellaSwag dataset: A challenging benchmark for commonsense inference that remains easy for humans (>95% accuracy) but is difficult for then state-of-the-art models (<48% accuracy).
- Adversarial Filtering (AF): A data collection method that iteratively uses discriminators to curate adversarial machine-generated distractors (wrong answers). AF scales the length and complexity of examples to a “Goldilocks zone,” where the generated options are obviously wrong to humans yet frequently fool models.
- Insight: Even powerful pretrained models can be overconfident or misled by surface patterns when confronted with carefully constructed adversarial examples, revealing gaps in genuine commonsense reasoning.
- Broader impact: Proposes that benchmarks should co-evolve adversarially alongside models to remain difficult and diagnostic as the state of the art advances.

Why it matters
- Demonstrates that high scores on existing tests do not imply human-level commonsense.
- Provides a methodology (AF) and dataset (HellaSwag) that better probe model robustness and reasoning.
- Encourages dynamic, adversarially-updated evaluation to avoid benchmark saturation.

Implementations and models referencing/associated with HellaSwag on Hugging Face
- google/gemma-7b
- google/gemma-7b-it
- google/gemma-2b
- google/gemma-2b-it
- google/gemma-2-2b
- google/gemma-2-2b-it
- google/gemma-2-9b
- google/gemma-2-9b-it
- google/gemma-2-27b-it
- google/gemma-3-270m
- google/gemma-3-270m-it
- google/gemma-3-1b-it
- google/gemma-3-4b-it
- google/gemma-3-12b-it
- google/gemma-3-27b-it
- google/gemma-3n-E4B-it
- google/gemma-3n-E4B-it-litert-preview
- google/gemma-3n-E2B-it-litert-preview
- google/vaultgemma-1b (entry truncated in source)

Core quantitative takeaway
- Humans: >95% accuracy on HellaSwag.
- State-of-the-art models at the time: <48% accuracy.

Bottom line
HellaSwag, built via Adversarial Filtering, exposes significant commonsense reasoning gaps in strong pretrained models, advocating for adversarially co-evolving benchmarks to meaningfully track progress.
Tool `Hugging Face:get-paper-info` with Parameter {'arxiv_id': '2109.07958'} on Hugging Face succeeded. Result (summarized from 344676 tokens): Title: TruthfulQA: Measuring How Models Mimic Human Falsehoods
Authors: Stephanie Lin, Jacob Hilton, Owain Evans
Link: https://huggingface.co/papers/2109.07958
ArXiv: 2109.07958

Core contribution
- Proposes TruthfulQA, a benchmark to evaluate whether language models generate truthful answers rather than repeating common human falsehoods.
- Dataset: 817 questions across 38 categories (e.g., health, law, finance, politics).
- Questions are crafted so that some humans answer falsely due to misconceptions; good performance requires avoiding false answers learned from imitating human text.

Evaluation
- Models tested: GPT-3, GPT-Neo/J, GPT-2, and a T5-based model.
- Best model truthfulness: 58% of questions.
- Human performance: 94%.
- Models often generated false answers mirroring popular misconceptions, with potential to deceive users.
- Larger models were generally less truthful—opposite of typical scaling trends in NLP—consistent with learning falsehoods from web text.

Implications
- Simply scaling model size is unlikely to improve truthfulness.
- Fine-tuning with objectives beyond imitation of web text is recommended to improve truthful behavior.

Implementations / Referencing models on Hugging Face (examples)
- Google Gemma family:
  - gemma-2b, gemma-2b-it, gemma-7b, gemma-7b-it (plus GGUF variants)
  - gemma-2 series: gemma-2-2b, gemma-2-2b-it, gemma-2-9b, gemma-2-9b-it, gemma-2-27b, gemma-2-27b-it
- Google RecurrentGemma: recurrentgemma-2b, recurrentgemma-2b-it, recurrentgemma-9b, recurrentgemma-9b-it
- Mistral: mistralai/Mistral-Small-24B-Base-2501
- Llama-based: akjindal53244/Llama-3.1-Storm-8B and GGUF variant
- Other references: MayaPH/GodziLLa2-70B; ILSP Meltemi models (Meltemi-7B-v1, Meltemi-7B-Instruct-v1)

Key numbers to remember
- 817 questions, 38 categories
- Best model: 58% truthful
- Humans: 94% truthful
- Larger models tended to be less truthful on this benchmark

Bottom line
TruthfulQA highlights that LMs frequently reproduce human misconceptions. Countering this likely requires targeted fine-tuning and training objectives focused on truthfulness, not just larger models or web-text imitation.
