Tool `Hugging Face:get-paper-info` with Parameter {'arxiv_id': '2405.16406'} on Hugging Face succeeded. Result (summarized from 177427 tokens): Title: SpinQuant: LLM quantization with learned rotations

Authors: Zechun Liu, Changsheng Zhao, Igor Fedorov, Bilge Soran, Dhruv Choudhary, Raghuraman Krishnamoorthi, Vikas Chandra, Yuandong Tian, Tijmen Blankevoort

Problem: Post-training quantization (PTQ) of LLMs reduces memory, latency, and power but suffers from large errors due to activation/weight outliers.

Key ideas and contributions:
- Rotation invariances in Transformers: The authors identify a family of rotation parameterizations that leave full-precision Transformer outputs unchanged, enabling rotations to be inserted without affecting FP accuracy.
- Random rotations are not equal: Different random rotations can yield up to a 13-point spread in downstream zero-shot reasoning accuracy after quantization.
- SpinQuant: Proposes learning orthogonal rotation matrices with Cayley optimization on a small validation set to remove outliers and improve quantization robustness.
- Unified 4-bit quantization: Applies 4-bit quantization to weights, activations, and the KV cache.

Results:
- LLaMA‑2 7B: With 4-bit quantization for weights, activations, and KV cache, SpinQuant narrows the zero-shot reasoning accuracy gap to full precision to 2.9 points.
- Outperforms PTQ/QAT baselines: On LLaMA‑2 7B, SpinQuant surpasses LLM‑QAT by 19.1 points and SmoothQuant by 25.0 points.
- Versus QuaRot (random rotations): SpinQuant reduces the gap to full precision by 30.2% (LLaMA‑2 7B) and 34.1% (LLaMA‑3 8B), improving on concurrent random-rotation methods.

Why it works:
- Learned orthogonal rotations reduce outlier magnitude in weight/activation distributions, directly benefiting PTQ.
- Cayley optimization efficiently enforces orthogonality and is trained on a small validation set, avoiding heavy retraining.

Scope:
- Focused on post-training, inference-time efficiency for LLMs.
- Demonstrated on challenging models (e.g., LLaMA‑2 7B, LLaMA‑3 8B).

Implementations (examples on Hugging Face):
- meta-llama/Llama-3.2-1B-Instruct-SpinQuant_INT4_EO8
- meta-llama/Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct-SpinQuant_INT4_EO8
- executorch-community/Llama-3.2-1B-Instruct-SpinQuant_INT4_EO8-ET
- executorch-community/Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct-SpinQuant_INT4_EO8-ET

Paper link: https://huggingface.co/papers/2405.16406

Takeaway:
Learning orthogonal rotations tailored to each model layer (via Cayley optimization) substantially improves 4-bit PTQ of weights, activations, and KV cache, closing most of the accuracy gap to full precision and outperforming SmoothQuant, LLM-QAT, and random-rotation baselines like QuaRot.
Tool `Hugging Face:get-paper-info` with Parameter {'arxiv_id': '2302.13971'} on Hugging Face succeeded. Result (summarized from 491943 tokens): Title: LLaMA: Open and Efficient Foundation Language Models
URL: https://huggingface.co/papers/2302.13971
Authors: Hugo Touvron; Thibaut Lavril; Gautier Izacard; Xavier Martinet; Marie-Anne Lachaux; Timothée Lacroix; Baptiste Rozière; Naman Goyal; Eric Hambro; Faisal Azhar; Aurelien Rodriguez; Armand Joulin; Edouard Grave; Guillaume Lample

Core contribution and results:
- Introduces LLaMA, a family of foundation language models ranging from 7B to 65B parameters.
- Trained on trillions of tokens using only publicly available datasets (no proprietary/inaccessible data).
- Performance:
  - LLaMA-13B outperforms GPT-3 (175B) on most benchmarks.
  - LLaMA-65B is competitive with state-of-the-art models such as Chinchilla-70B and PaLM-540B.
- Availability: all models are released to the research community.

Ecosystem and implementations referencing the work (selection):
- Broad adoption across open-source model families and fine-tunes, often citing arXiv:2302.13971. Examples include:
  - MosaicML MPT series:
    - mosaicml/mpt-7b (high community adoption; likes ~1174)
    - mosaicml/mpt-30b
    - mosaicml/mpt-1b-redpajama-200b (+ “-dolly” variant)
  - OpenLM “open_llama” series:
    - openlm-research/open_llama_3b; 3b_v2; 7b; 7b_v2; 13b; 13b_easylm
  - LMSYS Vicuna series:
    - lmsys/vicuna-7b-delta-v0; 7b-delta-v1.1; 7b-v1.1; 7b-v1.3
    - lmsys/vicuna-13b-delta-v0; 13b-delta-v1.1; 13b-v1.1; 13b-v1.3
    - lmsys/vicuna-33b-v1.3
  - Guanaco derivatives:
    - timdettmers/guanaco-65b; TheBloke/guanaco-65B-GGML; TheBloke/guanaco-33B-GGML
  - OLMo (AllenAI):
    - allenai/OLMo-7B; allenai/OLMo-1B
  - Instruction/chat and derivative models:
    - CarperAI/stable-vicuna-13b-delta; TheBloke/stable-vicuna-13B-HF; TheBloke/stable-vicuna-13B-GPTQ; TheBloke/stable-vicuna-13B-GGML
    - Open-Orca/OpenOrca
    - pankajmathur/orca_mini_v3_7b; orca_mini_v2_7b
    - cyberagent/calm2-7b-chat (Japanese/English)
    - stabilityai/StableBeluga1-Delta
    - pfnet/plamo-13b
    - xverse/XVERSE-65B
- Common characteristics across these implementations:
  - Primarily Transformers-based, PyTorch, and focused on text generation/causal language modeling.
  - Many trained or fine-tuned on large, public corpora (e.g., RedPajama-Data-1T, The Stack, C4/mC4, S2ORC, Dolma), reflecting LLaMA’s emphasis on public data.
  - Frequent availability under open licenses (Apache-2.0 common; some CC or other licenses), with safetensors and quantized variants (e.g., GPTQ, GGML) for efficient inference.
  - Strong community engagement (some models with very high likes and downloads), demonstrating widespread practical impact and downstream use of LLaMA-like architectures and training recipes.

Summary:
LLaMA establishes that high-performing large language models can be trained efficiently using only public data, delivering strong results at parameter counts from 7B to 65B. The 13B model surpasses GPT-3 (175B) on most benchmarks, and the 65B model is competitive with top contemporaries (Chinchilla-70B, PaLM-540B). Its release catalyzed a robust open-source ecosystem across base, instruction-tuned, multilingual, and quantized variants, widely adopted and extended by the community.


