Tool `Hugging Face:get-paper-info` with Parameter {'arxiv_id': '2307.08691'} on Hugging Face succeeded. Result (summarized from 53111 tokens): Title: FlashAttention-2: Faster Attention with Better Parallelism and Work Partitioning
Author: Tri Dao
Link: https://huggingface.co/papers/2307.08691 (arXiv: 2307.08691)

Core problem
- Transformer attention scales quadratically in sequence length for both runtime and memory, limiting long-context scaling across language, vision, code, audio, and video.
- FlashAttention (v1) leveraged the asymmetric GPU memory hierarchy to achieve linear memory usage and 2–4x speedups without approximation, but still reached only 25–40% of peak FLOPs/s due to suboptimal GPU work partitioning.

FlashAttention-2: contributions and methods
- Improves parallelism and work partitioning to better utilize GPU hardware:
  1) Algorithmic tweaks to reduce non-matmul FLOPs.
  2) Parallelize attention computation for a single head across multiple thread blocks to increase occupancy.
  3) Within each block, distribute work across warps to reduce shared-memory reads/writes and inter-warp communication.
- Results:
  - ~2x speedup over FlashAttention v1.
  - Achieves 50–73% of theoretical maximum FLOPs/s on NVIDIA A100, approaching GEMM efficiency.
  - End-to-end GPT-style training throughput up to 225 TFLOPs/s per A100 (72% model FLOPs utilization).
- Maintains exactness (no approximation) and linear memory scaling w.r.t. sequence length.

Context and significance
- Enables faster and more memory-efficient training/inference at long sequence lengths.
- Bridges the efficiency gap between attention and optimized matrix-multiply (GEMM) operations.

Implementations and usage on Hugging Face (selection; many cite arXiv:2307.08691)
- Falcon family:
  - tiiuae/falcon-11B (Transformers).
  - GGUF ports: LoneStriker/falcon-11B-GGUF, QuantFactory/falcon-11B-GGUF, vsevolodl/falcon-11B-GGUF, RichardErkhov/tiiuae_-_falcon-11B-gguf.
- Llama/Gemma derivatives and quantized variants:
  - amirMohammadi/Dorna-Llama3-8B-Instruct-Quantized4Bit (4-bit).
  - PJMixers-Dev Gemma-3 series (full and QLoRA/PEFT adapters), e.g.:
    - PJMixers-Dev/Gemma-3-Earthen-Completion-v0.1-4B
    - PJMixers-Dev/Gemma-3-Earthen-v0.1-4B
    - PJMixers-Dev/Gemma-3-Earthen-v0.1-4B-QLoRA
    - PJMixers-Dev/Gemma-3-Earthen-v0.2-4B-QLoRA
- T5/Flash-T5:
  - CATIE-AQ/FAT5-small (flash_t5).
- Florence-2 (vision-language) variants referencing the paper among others:
  - PJMixers-Images/Florence-2-base-Castollux-v0.1 / v0.2 / v0.4 / v0.5.
- BERT-based:
  - frederic-sadrieh/BERTchen-v0.1, hybrid-BERTchen-v0.1, BERTchen-v0.1-C4.

Notes on breadth of adoption
- Models span multiple libraries (Transformers, PEFT), formats (safetensors, GGUF), precisions (4/8-bit), and modalities (text generation, image-to-text, feature extraction).
- Tags across models consistently cite arXiv:2307.08691, indicating widespread integration or compatibility with FlashAttention-2 optimizations.

Key takeaways
- FlashAttention-2 substantially improves attention efficiency via smarter GPU parallelism and work partitioning.
- Achieves near-GEMM utilization on A100, doubles speed over FlashAttention v1, and delivers strong end-to-end training throughput without approximations.
- Broadly adopted across prominent open-source models and formats.
Tool `Hugging Face:get-paper-info` with Parameter {'arxiv_id': '2210.09261'} on Hugging Face succeeded. Result (summarized from 313515 tokens): Title: Challenging BIG-Bench Tasks and Whether Chain-of-Thought Can Solve Them (arXiv:2210.09261)
URL: https://huggingface.co/papers/2210.09261

Authors: Mirac Suzgun, Nathan Scales, Nathanael Schärli, Sebastian Gehrmann, Yi Tay, Hyung Won Chung, Aakanksha Chowdhery, Quoc V. Le, Ed H. Chi, Denny Zhou, Jason Wei

Problem and context:
- BIG-Bench is a diverse evaluation suite aimed at tasks believed to be beyond current language models’ capabilities.
- Prior results: the best model in the BIG-Bench paper exceeded average human-rater performance on 65% of tasks via few-shot prompting.
- Open question: On which tasks do models fall short of average human performance, and are these tasks actually unsolvable by current LMs?

Contribution:
- Defines BIG-Bench Hard (BBH), a subset of 23 challenging tasks where prior LMs did not outperform average human raters.
- Investigates whether chain-of-thought (CoT) prompting can close the gap on BBH tasks.

Methods:
- Applies CoT prompting to BBH tasks for PaLM and Codex (code-davinci-002).
- Analyzes interaction between CoT and model scale, including scaling curves.

Key results:
- With CoT prompting:
  - PaLM surpasses average human-rater performance on 10 of 23 BBH tasks.
  - Codex (code-davinci-002) surpasses average human-rater performance on 17 of 23 BBH tasks.
- Many BBH tasks require multi-step reasoning; standard few-shot prompting (without CoT) substantially underestimates the best achievable performance.
- CoT + scale: CoT induces emergent task performance on several BBH tasks that otherwise show flat scaling without CoT.

Implications:
- Chain-of-thought prompting better captures the reasoning capabilities of current LMs on challenging, multi-step tasks than standard few-shot prompting.
- Model scaling combined with CoT can unlock performance gains that do not appear under conventional prompting.

Implementations and related resources on Hugging Face:
- Datasets:
  - lukaemon/bbh (BBH dataset)
  - maveriq/bigbenchhard
  - ArtificialAnalysis/big_bench_audio
- Models referencing or aligned with the work (selection; many variants/formats exist):
  - Llama-3.1-Storm-8B family and variants (transformers, GGUF, FP8, 4-bit, 8-bit, AWQ), by akjindal53244, QuantFactory, RichardErkhov, HoneyBadger2989, unsloth
  - deeplang-ai/LingoWhale-8B
  - EpistemeAI2/FireStorm-Llama-3.1-8B and QuantFactory GGUF variants
  - unsloth/Llama-3.1-Storm-8B and -bnb-4bit
  - tianyil1/MistralForCausalLM_Cal_DPO (built on Zephyr-7B)
  - doktorb’s spydazweb_ai_humanai GGUF models
  - smalinin/Llama-3.1-Storm-8B-q4f32_1-MLC (web-llm/MLC)
  - rtt4fb/LlamaCode-Codeforces-v1 (cites BBH among datasets)

Overall takeaway:
- BBH highlights where LMs lag behind humans under standard prompting.
- Chain-of-thought prompting substantially improves performance on these hard tasks, often surpassing average human raters, and reveals scaling-driven emergent abilities that are otherwise hidden.
Tool `Hugging Face:get-paper-info` with Parameter {'arxiv_id': '2103.03874'} on Hugging Face succeeded. Result (summarized from 166362 tokens): Title: Measuring Mathematical Problem Solving With the MATH Dataset (arXiv:2103.03874)
Authors: Dan Hendrycks, Collin Burns, Saurav Kadavath, Akul Arora, Steven Basart, Eric Tang, Dawn Song, Jacob Steinhardt
Link: https://huggingface.co/papers/2103.03874

What the paper introduces
- MATH dataset: 12,500 challenging competition mathematics problems designed to measure mathematical problem solving in machine learning models.
- Each problem includes a full, step-by-step solution to enable training models to generate derivations and explanations.
- An additional large auxiliary pretraining corpus is provided to teach mathematical fundamentals and improve performance on MATH.

Key findings
- Despite targeted pretraining and very large Transformer models, accuracy on MATH remains relatively low.
- If current scaling trends continue, simply increasing model size and training budget appears impractical for achieving strong mathematical reasoning on this benchmark.
- Progress on mathematical problem solving likely requires new algorithmic advances beyond scale.

Why it matters
- Many intellectual tasks rely on robust mathematical reasoning; MATH offers a rigorous, solution-annotated benchmark for evaluating and training models on such skills.
- The dataset highlights limits of current scaling strategies and motivates research into reasoning-focused methods.

Implementations and references on Hugging Face (examples)
Numerous models list this paper in their tags (arxiv:2103.03874), especially across Google’s Gemma 2/3 families and one Mistral model. Representative entries include:
