Tool `National Parks:getParkDetails` with Parameter {'parkCode': 'yose'} on National Parks succeeded. Result (summarized from 3057 tokens): Yosemite National Park (code: yose)
- URL: https://www.nps.gov/yose/index.htm
- Designation: National Park
- Location: California (CA)
- Overview: First protected in 1864. Nearly 1,200 square miles in the Sierra Nevada. Famous for waterfalls, with deep valleys, grand meadows, ancient giant sequoias, vast wilderness.
- Coordinates: 37.84883288, -119.5571873
- Mailing address: PO Box 577, Yosemite National Park, CA 95389

Weather
- Elevation: ~2,000–13,000 ft (600–4,000 m).
- Precipitation: ~95% Oct–May (over 75% Nov–Mar).
- Snow: Most of park snow-covered Nov–May; Yosemite Valley may be rainy or snowy during winter storms.

Directions
- Year-round west entrances: Highways 41, 140, 120.
- East entrance (Tioga Pass via Hwy 120): Typically closed Nov through late May or June.
- Hetch Hetchy: Open year-round; may close intermittently due to snow.
- Note: GPS may be inaccurate in/around the park.
- Directions info: http://www.nps.gov/yose/planyourvisit/driving.htm

Contacts
- Phone: 209/372-0200
- Email: yose_web_manager@nps.gov

Entrance Fees (valid 7 consecutive days unless noted)
- Entrance – Private Vehicle: $35. Non-commercial car/pickup/RV/van ≤15 seats; covers vehicle and occupants.
- Entrance – Motorcycle: $30. Per motorcycle.
- Entrance – Per Person: $20. For foot, bicycle, horse, or non-commercial bus/van; ages 15 and under free.
- Commercial Entrance – Sedan: $25 + $20 per person (max $105). CUA required.
- Commercial Entrance – Van: $125. CUA required.
- Commercial Entrance – Mini-bus: $200. CUA required.
- Commercial Entrance – Motor Coach: $300. CUA required.
- Entrance – Non-commercial Groups: $20 per person, max $300 per vehicle; ages 15 and under free.
- Entrance – Education/Academic Groups: $0. Fee waiver available for accredited/tax-exempt groups on educational/scientific trips.
- Commercial Entrance – Per Person: $20. (Fee structure note: $25 + $20 per person, not to exceed $105; CUA required.)

Entrance Passes
- Annual Entrance – Park: $70. Free entrance to Yosemite for 12 months from purchase.

Operating Hours
- Yosemite National Park: Open 24/7, 365 days/year.
- Hetch Hetchy: Open sunrise to sunset (except permitted backpackers). All day visitors must exit by sunset. Road closed to all vehicles from sunset to sunrise.

Topics
- Culture/History: Architecture and Building; African American Heritage; Archeology; Arts; Painting; Photography; Asian American Heritage; Burial/Cemeteries; Native American Heritage; Religion/Spirituality; Churches; Schools and Education; Women’s History; Tragic Events (Floods); Transportation; Science/Technology/Innovation.
- Nature: Animals (Birds, Cats [wild], Fish); Climate Change; Fire; Foothills/Plains/Valleys; Forests/Woodlands; Geology; Glaciers; Grasslands; Meadows; Lakes; Mountains; Natural Sounds; Night Sky/Astronomy; Rivers/Riparian; Rock Landscapes/Features; Scenic Views; Trails; Unique Species; Watersheds; Waterfalls; Wetlands; Wilderness.

Activities
- Arts and Culture; Auto/ATV; Scenic Driving; Astronomy; Biking; Boating; Camping (including Backcountry); Climbing; Fishing; Food; Guided Tours; Hands-On; Hiking (including Backcountry); Horse Trekking/Horseback Riding; Ice Skating; Living History; Paddling; Junior Ranger Program; Tubing/Snow Tubing; Skiing; Snow Play; Snowshoeing; Swimming; Wildlife Watching/Birdwatching; Park Film; Museum Exhibits; Shopping (Bookstore/Park Store).

Images (sample highlights; credit NPS unless noted)
- Yosemite Falls on a Winter Morning: Two tall waterfalls on snow-covered granite; winter flows vary. Credit: NPS / Cindy Jacoby. URL: https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C84CC4C-1DD8-B71B-0BE967E5E5D93F25.jpg
- Upper Yosemite Fall and Merced River in spring. NPS Photo. URL: https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/05383E91-AA28-2DDC-AB517507594F9FA6.jpg
- Rainbow over Half Dome: View from Sentinel Dome. Credit: NPS / Bob Roney. URL: https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C84C6CF-1DD8-B71B-0B1C7CB883AA8FB1.
Tool `National Parks:getParkDetails` with Parameter {'parkCode': 'seki'} on National Parks succeeded. Result (summarized from 2778 tokens): Sequoia & Kings Canyon National Parks (code: seki)
- URL: https://www.nps.gov/seki/index.htm
- Designation: National Parks
- State: CA
- Description: Huge mountains, rugged foothills, deep canyons, vast caverns, and the world’s largest trees showcase diverse landscapes, life, and beauty. Learn about plants, animals, and the threats they face. Ancient giant sequoias, though massive, are vulnerable.

Weather
- Wide variation with elevation.
- Summer: Comfortable in sequoia groves; hot/dry in foothills; snow lingers on high passes into summer.
- Fall: Conditions range from summer-like to winter-like.
- Winter: Snow in sequoia groves; rain in foothills; be prepared for tire chains.
- Spring: Great foothill hiking; snowmelt begins at higher elevations.

Directions
- Highways: Hwy 180 from Fresno enters Kings Canyon NP and continues ~30 miles east to Cedar Grove. Hwy 198 from Visalia enters Sequoia NP via Three Rivers.
- Inside parks: Hwy 198 becomes Generals Highway, connecting 198 to 180.
- Vehicle advisory: Over 22 ft should enter via Hwy 180.
- Winter: Generals Highway between the parks often closes. Chains may be required. No roads cross the parks east–west.
- Directions URL: http://www.nps.gov/seki/planyourvisit/directions.htm

Location
- Coordinates: 36.71277299, -118.587429
- Address: 47050 Generals Highway, Three Rivers, CA 93271

Contacts
- Phone (Voice): 559-565-3341
- Fax: 559-565-3730
- Email: SEKI_information@nps.gov

Entrance Fees (valid for Sequoia & Kings Canyon NPs and Hume Lake District of Sequoia National Forest/Giant Sequoia National Monument unless noted)
- Entrance – Private Vehicle: $35.00; valid 1–7 days; all occupants in a single vehicle.
- Entrance – Per Person: $20.00; valid 1–7 days; on foot/bicycle.
- Entrance – Motorcycle: $30.00; valid 1–7 days; all riders on motorcycle/similar.
- Entrance – Non-commercial Groups: $35 per vehicle (capacity up to 15) or applicable pass; vehicles with capacity 16+ charged $20 per person, up to the commercial maximums ($100 or $200). Ages 15 and under, passholders, and drivers hired for transportation only are exempt from per-person fee.
- Commercial Entrance – Sedan: $25 per vehicle + $20 per passenger; max $75. Fee based on vehicle seating capacity (1–6).
- Commercial Entrance – Per Person: $20 per person + $25 per vehicle; max $75 (for vehicles seating 1–6).
- Commercial Entrance – Van: $75.00 (seating 7–15).
- Commercial Entrance – Mini-bus: $100.00 (seating 16–25).
- Commercial Entrance – Motor Coach: $200.00 (seating 26+).
(Note: Commercial fees are based on seating capacity, not actual passengers; ages and passes do not change the price.)

Entrance Passes
- Annual Entrance – Park: $70.00; valid one year from month of purchase; admits all passengers in a private vehicle; non-transferable; valid for Sequoia & Kings Canyon NPs and Hume Lake Ranger District of Sequoia National Forest/Giant Sequoia National Monument.

Operating Hours
- Open 24 hours/day, 365 days/year.
- Some park and access roads close in winter due to snow; rare extreme weather may close entry roads.

Topics
- African American Heritage; Arts (Painting, Photography, Poetry/Literature); Transportation (Roads, Routes and Highways); Animals (Birds); Caves/Caverns/Karst; Canyons/Canyonlands; Climate Change; Fire; Foothills/Plains/Valleys; Forests/Woodlands (Coniferous Forests); Geology; Grasslands; Meadows; Lakes; Mountains; Natural Sounds; Night Sky/Astronomy; River/Riparian; Rock Landscapes/Features; Scenic Views; Trails; Unique/Rare/Endangered Species; Watersheds/Headwaters; Wilderness.

Activities
- Auto/ATV (Scenic Driving); Astronomy/Stargazing; Camping (Backcountry, Front-country/Car, Horse/Stock, Group, RV); Caving; Climbing/Rock Climbing; Fishing (Freshwater, Fly); Food (Dining, Picnicking); Guided Tours; Hands-On (Citizen Science); Hiking (Backcountry, Front-country); Horse Trekking/Horseback Riding; Junior Ranger Program; Winter: Cross-country Skiing, Snow Play, Snowshoeing; Wildlife Watching/B
Tool `National Parks:getParkDetails` with Parameter {'parkCode': 'deva'} on National Parks succeeded. Result (summarized from 2751 tokens): - Name: Death Valley National Park (code: deva)
- URL: https://www.nps.gov/deva/index.htm
- Designation: National Park
- States: CA, NV
- Description: Below-sea-level basin known for extreme drought and record summer heat; contrasts include snow-frosted peaks, rare wildflower blooms after rains, and oases that support diverse life.

Weather
- Spring: Most popular; warm/sunny; possible wildflowers.
- Summer: Starts early; scorching by May; extreme heat common.
- Autumn: From late October; warm, pleasant, clear.
- Winter: Cool days, chilly nights, rare rain; snow on high peaks; excellent light for exploration.

Directions
- Info: Multiple park entrances; see link for best travel routes.
- Directions URL: http://www.nps.gov/deva/planyourvisit/directions.htm

Location
- Latitude/Longitude: 36.48753731, -117.134395
- Address: PO Box 579, Death Valley, CA 92328 (State: CA)

Contacts
- Phone (Voice): 760-786-3200
- Email: deva_information@nps.gov

Entrance Fees (7-day unless noted)
- Entrance – Private Vehicle: $30. Allows re-entry for all in one non-commercial vehicle (car/truck/van/RV/aircraft).
- Entrance – Motorcycle: $25. Allows re-entry for one motorcycle.
- Entrance – Per Person: $15. One individual on foot or one bicycle; re-entry allowed.
- Commercial Entrance – Per Person: $10. 1–6 passenger vehicle: $25 + $10 per person. Additional permits required for commercial groups, special uses, film/photography, research, etc.
- Commercial Entrance – Sedan: $25. 1–6 passenger vehicle: $25 + $10 per person; additional permits may be required.
- Commercial Entrance – Van: $75. 7–15 passengers; additional permits may be required.
- Commercial Entrance – Mini-bus: $100. 16–25 passengers; additional permits may be required.
- Commercial Entrance – Motor Coach: $200. 26+ passengers; additional permits may be required.
- Entrance – Non-commercial Groups: $15 per person in vehicles with capacity 16+; not to exceed $200.
- Entrance – Education/Academic Groups: $0 with approved fee waiver for educational/scientific visits; submit at least 1 month prior.

Entrance Passes
- Annual Entrance – Park: $55. Valid 12 months from month of purchase; covers all persons with pass holder in one private non-commercial vehicle or motorcycle.

Operating Hours
- Park is open daily, year-round (All Day: Sunday–Saturday).

Topics
- Archeology; Asian American Heritage; Incarceration; Japanese-American Internment; Industry; Mining; Native American Heritage; Westward Expansion; The Gold Rush; Women's History; Animals; Birds; Fish; Tortoises and Turtles; Dunes; Foothills, Plains and Valleys; Fossils and Paleontology; Geology; Mountains; Night Sky; Astronomy; Scenic Views; Trails; Unique Species; Endangered; Wilderness.

Activities
- Astronomy, Stargazing; Biking (Mountain, Road); Camping (Backcountry, Car/Front Country); Canyoneering; Food; Flying; Golf; Guided Tours; Hiking (Backcountry, Front-Country); Horse Trekking; Living History (First Person Interpretation); Junior Ranger Program; Wildlife Watching (Birdwatching); Park Film; Museum Exhibits; Shopping (Bookstore/Park Store, Gift Shop/Souvenirs).

Images
