Tool `National Parks:getParkDetails` with Parameter {'parkCode': 'redw'} on National Parks succeeded. Result (summarized from 3639 tokens): Redwood National and State Parks (code: redw)
- Website: https://www.nps.gov/redw/index.htm
- Designation: National and State Parks
- State: CA
- Description: Home to the world’s tallest trees and diverse landscapes—from prairies and oak woodlands to rivers and rugged coastline. These ancestral lands have supported people for thousands of years. Managed jointly by the National Park Service and California State Parks to protect and restore resources for enjoyment, learning, and inspiration.

Location and Directions
- Latitude, Longitude: 41.37237268, -124.0318129
- Address: 1111 Second Street, Crescent City, CA 95531
- Region: Northernmost coastal California, near the Oregon border; park corridor ~60 miles long with four visitor centers north-to-south.
- Driving distances: ~325 miles (6–7 hours) north of San Francisco; ~330 miles (6 hours) south of Portland, OR; ~170 miles (4 hours) west of Redding, CA.
- Directions page: http://www.nps.gov/redw/planyourvisit/directions.htm

Contacts
- Phone (Voice): 7074646101
- Fax: 7074641812
- Email: redw_info@nps.gov

Hours and Fees
- Park access: Open year-round, 24/7 for roads, trails, and public access.
- Seasonal notes: Some campgrounds and visitor centers are closed or have reduced hours October–May.
- Entrance fees/passes: None listed.

Weather
- Be prepared for cool, damp, and wet conditions; dress in layers.
- Typical temps along the redwood coast year-round: mid-40s°F (7°C) to mid-60s°F (18°C).
- Summer: Often foggy; highs occasionally in low 70s°F (20°C).
- Winter: Cooler with considerable rain.
- Rainfall (Oct–Apr): Averages 60–80 inches across the region.

Topics
- Human history: Colonization and Settlement; Farming and Agriculture; Ranches; Industry; Mills; Logging and Timber Cutting; Maritime; Coastal Defenses; Native American Heritage; Social Movements; Conservation Movement; Transportation; Roads, Routes and Highways; Tragic Events; Massacres; Wars and Conflicts; Tribal Conflicts; Indian and Frontier Wars; Westward Expansion; Homesteading.
- Natural resources: Animals; Birds; Cats (wild); Elk; Fish; Climate Change; Fire; Forests and Woodlands; Coniferous Forests; Geology; Groundwater; Grasslands; Prairies; Oceans; Whales; River and Riparian; Scenic Views; Unique Species; Rare; Endangered; Watersheds; Headwaters; Wilderness.

Activities
