2025-12-01 12:05:12,721 - benchmark.runner - INFO - Here are the concrete, checked conversions and the physics tying temperature, density, and pressure together, based on your starting values (T = 25°C, density = 1 g/cm³, pressure = 101.325 kPa):

Key unit conversions (with calculations)
- Temperature: 25°C → Kelvin
  - Formula: T(K) = t(°C) + 273.15
  - Result: 25 + 273.15 = 298.15 K
  - Source: BIPM SI Brochure (Celsius and Kelvin relationship)

- Density: 1 g/cm³ → other units
  - To kg/m³:
    - 1 g = 1×10⁻³ kg; 1 cm³ = (10⁻² m)³ = 1×10⁻⁶ m³
    - 1 g/cm³ = (1×10⁻³ kg)/(1×10⁻⁶ m³) = 1000 kg/m³
  - To g/L:
    - 1 cm³ = 1 mL = 1×10⁻³ L
    - 1 g/cm³ = 1 g/mL = 1000 g/L
  - Also useful: 1 g/cm³ = 1 kg/L = 1000 kg/m³ = 1000 g/L
  - Sources: SI base-unit relations (BIPM SI Brochure); liter definition 1 L = 1 dm³ = 10⁻³ m³ (BIPM/NIST)

- Pressure: 101.325 kPa → atm and psi
  - To atmospheres:
    - 1 atm = 101.325 kPa (exact convention)
    - 101.325 kPa = 1.000000 atm
    - Source: IUPAC Gold Book; NIST SP 811
  - To psi:
    - Exact factor: 1 psi = 6894.757293168 Pa
    - 101.325 kPa × (1000 Pa/kPa) / (6894.757293168 Pa/psi)
      = 101,325 / 6894.757293168
      = 14.6959487755 psi
    - Rounded: 14.69595 psi
    - Source: NIST SP 811 Appendix B.8 (inch and lbf exact definitions lead to exact psi↔Pa)

How temperature, density, and pressure are linked (ideal gas framework)
- Ideal gas relations:
  - PV = nRT and density ρ = mass/volume = (nM)/V ⇒ ρ = (P M)/(R T)
  - Equivalently: P = (ρ R T)/M
  - R (molar gas constant) = 8.314462618 J·mol⁻¹·K⁻¹
  - Source: NIST CODATA recommended constants

- What your numbers imply:
  - Using your stated density ρ = 1 g/cm³ = 1000 kg/m³ at T = 298.15 K and P = 101,325 Pa:
    - Implied molar mass M = (ρ R T)/P
    - = (1000 kg/m³ × 8.314462618 J·mol⁻¹·K⁻¹ × 298.15 K) / 101,325 Pa
    - = 24.4654 kg/mol = 24,465 g/mol
    - That molar mass is far too large for any ordinary gas at 1 atm, which strongly suggests the density you intended might be 1 g/L (not 1 g/cm³).
  - If the intended density was 1 g/L = 1 kg/m³ at 25°C and 1 atm:
    - M = (1 × 8.314462618 × 298.15) / 101,325 = 0.0244654 kg/mol = 24.465 g/mol
    - That is physically reasonable for a gas (on the order of tens of g/mol).

- How changing temperature affects ρ and P:
  - At constant pressure (P fixed): ρ ∝ 1/T. Example: from 25°C (298.15 K) to 35°C (308.15 K),
    - ρ₂ = ρ₁ × (T₁/T₂) = ρ₁ × (298.15/308.15) ≈ 0.9676 ρ₁ (about a 3.24% decrease).
  - At constant density (or constant n and V): P ∝ T. Same temperature change:
    - P₂ = P₁ × (T₂/T₁) = 101.325 kPa × (308.15/298.15) ≈ 104.73 kPa
    - In psi: 14.69595 psi × (308.15/298.15) ≈ 15.19 psi

Bottom line numbers for your starting point
- Temperature: 25°C = 298.15 K
- Density: 1 g/cm³ = 1000 kg/m³ = 1000 g/L = 1 kg/L
- Pressure: 101.325 kPa = 1 atm = 14.69595 psi
- Ideal-gas linkage: P = (ρ R T)/M and ρ = (P M)/(R T)
  - With ρ = 1 g/cm³ at 25°C and 1 atm, the implied M ≈ 24,465 g/mol (unphysical for a gas). Please verify whether the intended density was 1 g/L; if so, the implied M ≈ 24.47 g/mol (reasonable).

Authoritative sources (for verification)
- Celsius ↔ Kelvin relation: BIPM SI Brochure (Celsius temperature t and thermodynamic temperature T), https://www.bipm.org/en/publications/si-brochure
- Liter and SI unit relations (1 L = 1 dm³ = 10⁻³ m³; 1 cm = 10⁻² m; 1 g = 10⁻³ kg): BIPM SI Brochure; NIST SP 811 Guide to the SI, https://www.nist.gov/pml/special-publication-811
- Standard atmosphere: 1 atm = 101,325 Pa (exact), IUPAC Gold Book, https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/S05921
- psi conversion: 1 in = 0.0254 m (exact), 1 lbf = 4.4482216152605 N (derived from exact definitions), yielding 1 psi = 6894.757293168 Pa; NIST SP 811 Appendix B.8 Conversion Factors, https://www.nist.gov/pml/special-publication-811/nist-guide-si-appendix-b-8-conversion-factors
- Molar gas constant R: R = 8.314462618 J·mol⁻¹·K⁻¹ (from exact kB and NA), NIST CODATA, https://physics.nist.gov/cgi-bin/cuu/Value?R

If you confirm the correct density units (g/cm³ vs g/L), I can plug in your actual value to compute the gas’s molar mass or, given M, compute the expected density at any temperature and pressure you choose.
